{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Meromorphic.Divisor","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Meromorphic.Order","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Meromorphic.FactorizedRational","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.LogDeriv","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.CauchyIntegral","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.CircleIntegral"],"declarations":13,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.entire_zero_count","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.entire_zero_count","kind":"theorem","line":641,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L641-L685","doc":"**Zero count of an entire function (canonical-product application).** For `f`\nentire (`Differentiable ℂ f`) — in particular a Hadamard canonical product (PI-1) —\nthere are no poles, so `divisor ≥ 0` and the argument principle counts *zeros*:\n`(2πi)⁻¹ ∮_{C(c,R)} f'/f = zeroCount = #zeros in B(c,R)` with multiplicity.\n\nThis is the PI-3 payload for the pentagon program: it makes the number of zeros of\nthe canonical minimal Faddeev product `phiBProd` inside a disc an explicit contour\nintegral, the tool that pins `phiBProd` among its elliptic multiples.\n\n**PROVED fully sorry-free** — a specialization of\n`argument_principle` to the entire (pole-free) case. `f` entire ⇒ analytic\neverywhere (so the `hfU` hypothesis is free) and `meromorphicOrderAt f w ≥ 0` at\nevery `w` (`AnalyticAt.meromorphicOrderAt_nonneg`), hence `divisor f ≥ 0` on `S`, so\n`Σ_{w∈S} divisor w = zeroCount`. `hfne` (no zeros off `S`) is passed through — it is\nthe precise \"`S` enumerates all zeros in the disc\" hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.argument_principle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.argument_principle","kind":"theorem","line":456,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L456-L637","doc":"**The argument principle.** For `f` meromorphic on the closed disc\n`\\overline B(c,R)`, analytic and nonvanishing off a finite set `S ⊆ B(c,R)` of\nzeros/poles (`hfU`, `hfne` — the precise \"no zeros or poles other than `S`, and none\non the boundary circle\" hypotheses), the contour integral of `f'/f` counts the\ninterior divisor:\n`(2πi)⁻¹ ∮_{C(c,R)} logDeriv f = Σ_{w∈S} ord_w f`,\nwhere `S` enumerates the finitely many interior points where the divisor is nonzero.\nCombined with `zeroCount_sub_poleCount_eq_sum_divisor`, the RHS is `#zeros − #poles`\nwith multiplicity.\n\n**PROVED fully sorry-free** via the global Weierstrass factorization\n`MeromorphicOn.extract_zeros_poles`: `f = (∏ᶠ_w (·-w)^{ord_w f}) · g` on the closed disc\nwith `g` analytic nonvanishing, so on the bounding circle\n`logDeriv f = Σ_{w∈S} (ord_w f)·(·-w)⁻¹ + logDeriv g`. Integrating,\n`∮_{C(c,R)} logDeriv f = Σ_{w∈S} (ord_w f)·2πi + 0`: each bare simple pole gives\n`∮_{C(c,R)} (z-w)⁻¹ = 2πi` for every interior `w`\n(`circleIntegral.integral_sub_inv_of_mem_ball` — off-centre, no deformation needed) and\nthe analytic remainder `logDeriv g` integrates to `0` by Cauchy–Goursat\n(`circleIntegral_logDeriv_eq_zero_of_analyticOnNhd`). Multiplying by `(2πi)⁻¹` gives the\ndivisor sum. No contour deformation is used. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.circleIntegral_sum_simple_poles","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.circleIntegral_sum_simple_poles","kind":"theorem","line":423,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L423-L452","doc":"**Sum-swap for a Finset of interior simple poles (sorry-free).** The contour integral\nof `Σ_{u∈T} a u·(z-u)⁻¹` over `C(c,R)` is `Σ_{u∈T} a u·2πi`, by\n`circleIntegral.integral_sub_inv_of_mem_ball` (the off-centre Cauchy pole integral) applied\nterm by term. This is the sum side of the argument principle's contour integral. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.circleIntegrable_sum_simple_poles","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.circleIntegrable_sum_simple_poles","kind":"theorem","line":402,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L402-L421","doc":"**Integrability of a Finset of simple poles on the bounding circle (sorry-free).**\nFor radii-free simple poles `Σ_{u∈T} a u·(z-u)⁻¹` with every `u ∈ T` interior to the\ndisc `B(c,R)`, the integrand is continuous on the bounding circle `C(c,R)` (no `u` lies\non it), hence circle-integrable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.residue_logDeriv_eq_order","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.residue_logDeriv_eq_order","kind":"theorem","line":258,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L258-L381","doc":"**Residue of the logarithmic derivative = order** (the local argument\nprinciple). For `f` meromorphic at `w` with finite order `n = ord_w f`, and all\nsufficiently small circles `C(w,r)` avoiding other zeros/poles, the small-circle\naverage of `f'/f` recovers the integer order:\n`(2πi)⁻¹ ∮_{C(w,r)} logDeriv f = n`.\n\nReason: near `w`, `f(z) = (z-w)^n·g(z)` with `g` analytic and nonvanishing\n(`meromorphicOrderAt_eq_int_iff`), so `logDeriv f = n/(z-w) + logDeriv g`; the\nanalytic `logDeriv g` integrates to `0` (Cauchy), and `∮ (z-w)⁻¹ = 2πi`\n(`circleIntegral_sub_center_inv_smul_of_differentiable_on_off_countable`), leaving\n`n`. Mathlib has BOTH ingredients but not this composition.\n\n**PROVED (sorry-free).** The factorization core is now discharged from the standard\nargument-principle hypotheses: `f` meromorphic at `w`, analytic and nonvanishing on\nthe punctured open neighborhood `U \\ {w}` of the closed disc. These are exactly the\nhypotheses a real argument-principle setup provides; the earlier pointwise-`hsmall`\nformulation was too weak to build an OPEN-neighborhood factor (an open `U ⊇\nclosedBall w r` necessarily reaches points outside the disc, where a pointwise-order\nhypothesis says nothing), so it is replaced here by the honest `AnalyticOnNhd`/\nno-other-zeros pair. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.circleIntegral_logDeriv_eq_zero_of_analyticOnNhd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.circleIntegral_logDeriv_eq_zero_of_analyticOnNhd","kind":"theorem","line":241,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L241-L256","doc":"**Cauchy–Goursat vanishing of the analytic part (brick 3).** For `g` analytic and\nnonvanishing on a neighborhood of the closed disc `\\overline B(w,r)`, its logarithmic\nderivative `logDeriv g = g'/g` is analytic there, hence integrates to `0` over the\nbounding circle (Cauchy–Goursat). Sorry-free, via\n`circleIntegral_eq_zero_of_differentiable_on_off_countable`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.inv_two_pi_I_mul_integral_nat_sub_center_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.inv_two_pi_I_mul_integral_nat_sub_center_inv","kind":"theorem","line":231,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L231-L239","doc":"**Cauchy simple-pole term (brick 2).** The normalized small-circle integral of\nthe simple pole `n·(z−w)⁻¹` recovers the integer `n`:\n`(2πi)⁻¹ ∮_{C(w,r)} n·(z−w)⁻¹ = n`. Direct from `circleIntegral.integral_sub_center_inv`\n— sorry-free. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.logDeriv_zpow_sub_mul_split","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.logDeriv_zpow_sub_mul_split","kind":"theorem","line":210,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L210-L229","doc":"**Log-derivative split at a zero/pole (brick 1).** If `f = (·−w)^n · g` locally\nwith `g` differentiable and nonvanishing at `z` and `z ≠ w`, then the logarithmic\nderivative splits into the simple-pole part `n·(z−w)⁻¹` and the analytic part\n`logDeriv g`. Pure `logDeriv` algebra — sorry-free. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.two_pi_I_ne_zero'","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.two_pi_I_ne_zero'","kind":"theorem","line":191,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L191-L197","doc":"**The Cauchy normalization `2πi ≠ 0`.** Makes the `(2πi)⁻¹` prefactor of the\nargument principle a genuine inverse (Mathlib: `Complex.two_pi_I_ne_zero`; reproved\nlocally to keep this file's cited API explicit). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.zeroCount_sub_poleCount_eq_sum_divisor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.zeroCount_sub_poleCount_eq_sum_divisor","kind":"theorem","line":178,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L178-L187","doc":"**The RHS of the argument principle, combinatorially.** For any ℤ-valued\ndivisor `D` and finite support set `S`, `#zeros − #poles = Σ ord`:\n`(zeroCount D S) − (poleCount D S) = ∑_{w∈S} D w`. This is the whole right-hand\nside, complete and analysis-free — the argument principle's remaining content is\nentirely the *left*-hand (contour-integral) side. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.poleCount","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.poleCount","kind":"def","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L173-L176","doc":"**Pole count**: the sum of the magnitudes of negative divisor values (orders of\npoles). With `S` = the divisor support inside a region, this is `#poles` counted\nwith multiplicity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.zeroCount","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.zeroCount","kind":"def","line":168,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L168-L171","doc":"**Zero count**: the sum of positive divisor values (orders of zeros) over a\nfinite set `S` of points. With `S` = the divisor's support inside a region, this is\n`#zeros` counted with multiplicity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.toNat_sub_toNat_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ArgumentPrinciple.toNat_sub_toNat_neg","kind":"theorem","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ArgumentPrinciple.lean#L161-L166","doc":"**The integer split identity** `n = n⁺ − n⁻`, i.e. `n.toNat − (−n).toNat = n`.\nFor `n ≥ 0` this is `n − 0`; for `n < 0` it is `0 − (−n)`. This is the per-point\nstatement that a divisor value (zero-order minus pole-order) decomposes into a\nzero contribution and a pole contribution. ","signatureChunk":0}]}